  "source_inspection": {
    "supported": [
      "The audited diff is narrowly scoped to ClassParser#try_package. It widens the identity and modifier regexes and concatenates identity-slot and trailing-slot stereotypes into one Rust vector; there is no renderer-side repair, no fixture-name branching, and no new numeric layout constants.",
      "The original five preserved follow-up inputs from class-package-style-header-separation now all pass exactly under the no-oracle helper, so the narrow two-trailing-stereotype ownership bug that motivated the account is genuinely fixed.",
      "Static review found no test-diagram reads, include_str/include_bytes additions, comparator changes, or coordinate-keyed SVG surgery in the audited implementation diff."
    ],
    "disproven": [
      "The account's claim that Java semantics preserve every visible stereotype across both the pre-AS identity slot and the trailing post-identity slot is false. In Java, CommandPackageWithUSymbol stores those captures under different names (STEREOTYPE1/2/3 and STEREOTYPE), while RegexResult#getLazzy returns the first prefix-matching capture and RegexComposed#createPartialMatch stores matches in a HashMap. Java does not concatenate both slots into one stereotype string.",
      "The mixed explicit split held-out checker_three_explicit_split_root_lr shows the consequence directly: Java renders only the trailing stereotype <<East>> above the title Split Explicit, while Rust renders <<North>>, <<South>>, and <<East>>, enlarging the cluster and changing text order.",
      "The mixed fallback split held-out checker_three_fallback_split_nested_tb disproves the blanket canonical-path invariant. Java's cluster and leaf are qualified as Host.Split Fallback and Host.Split Fallback.SplitLeaf, while Rust qualifies them as Host.SplitFallback and Host.SplitFallback.SplitLeaf."
    ]
  },
